# Runtime execution safety

The agent must **never** build, run, test, or watch BuildMonitor unless the user **explicitly asks in the current conversation**.

## Forbidden (always)

Do not invoke, schedule, or run in a terminal:

- `dotnet build`, `dotnet run`, `dotnet watch`, `dotnet test`
- `npm run`, `msbuild`, or scripts whose purpose is compile, run, or test
- Starting or stopping `BuildMonitor.TrayApp` or child `dotnet watch` processes for verification
- Reading the terminals folder to manage watch output
- `Await` on watch startup or compile output

**No exceptions** — not for UI work, ship checklist, or “just verify”. Give the user exact commands and wait for their output.

## Required instead

| Situation | Agent does |
|-----------|------------|
| Code edited | List files changed; stop when edits are done |
| User should verify compile | Provide commands below; ask user to paste errors if build fails |
| User should verify tray UI | Describe what to check in the status panel / settings after user confirms app is running |
| Tests needed | Print `dotnet test` command; user runs it |
| Ship / PR | User confirms build + test passed; no new warnings in changed files (`build-warnings.mdc`) |

## Commands (user runs)

```powershell
dotnet build BuildMonitor.slnx
dotnet test src/BuildMonitor.Tests/BuildMonitor.Tests.csproj
dotnet watch run --project src/TrayApp/BuildMonitor.TrayApp.csproj
```

## Cursor usage

See `credit-conservation.mdc`. Prefer **Chat** for review; **Agent** when files must change. User runs build/test; agent does not “verify” with extra tool rounds.
